(5845883, 847558, 103142, '<td valign="top">Comment:<br/>Good day! You''re the recipient of a "critique club" critique! So feel free to take this with a grain of salt :D<br/> <br/>First Impression: I think it definitely meets the challenge -- the structure of the ice is very interesting and very delicate. I like the contrast of the moving, blurred water and the structured ice.<br/> <br/>Composition: I''m really struggling with this one. It''s a very interesting photo and a wonderful find, yet it seems to fall a little flat, yet I''m not sure how to improve it. My first thought it POV. It would be a cold undertaking, but I''m thinking that, if possible, lying down flat and taking the picture more from the level of the ice (or closer to that level) might make it more dynamic. Or perhaps cropping in from the right. The rocks aren''t as interesting, and it would bring out the diagonal of the water flow a tad more.<br/> <br/>Subject: Good subject, delicate, interesting, intriguing. Did you try a couple of shots zooming in and isolating the ice crystals? Both the skirt on the right rock and the curtain of ice in the back are quite fascinating. <br/> <br/>Technical: I think you did well on the technicals. Good blur on the water, nice and crisp on the ice. I''m glad you didn''t over sharpen or over process this. It''s the type of shot that needs to be natural! It might benefit from a little more contrast. I''d try playing with the levels and bring out a little more darkness in the rocks -- it could set the ice and water off even more effectively.<br/> <br/>Final thoughts: Interesting shot - lovely spot. Nice choice for this challenge.</td>', 'Comment:Good day! You''re the recipient of a "critique club" critique! So feel free to take this with a grain of salt :D First Impression: I think it definitely meets the challenge -- the structure of the ice is very interesting and very delicate. I like the contrast of the moving, blurred water and the structured ice. Composition: I''m really struggling with this one. It''s a very interesting photo and a wonderful find, yet it seems to fall a little flat, yet I''m not sure how to improve it. My first thought it POV. It would be a cold undertaking, but I''m thinking that, if possible, lying down flat and taking the picture more from the level of the ice (or closer to that level) might make it more dynamic. Or perhaps cropping in from the right. The rocks aren''t as interesting, and it would bring out the diagonal of the water flow a tad more. Subject: Good subject, delicate, interesting, intriguing. Did you try a couple of shots zooming in and isolating the ice crystals? Both the skirt on the right rock and the curtain of ice in the back are quite fascinating.  Technical: I think you did well on the technicals. Good blur on the water, nice and crisp on the ice. I''m glad you didn''t over sharpen or over process this. It''s the type of shot that needs to be natural! It might benefit from a little more contrast. I''d try playing with the levels and bring out a little more darkness in the rocks -- it could set the ice and water off even more effectively. Final thoughts: Interesting shot - lovely spot. Nice choice for this challenge.', '2010-01-31 22:51:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(5846225, 848100, 65779, '<td valign="top">Yes 1/60s is utter nonsense for this.</td>', 'Yes 1/60s is utter nonsense for this.', '2010-02-01 00:59:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847396, 847813, 112107, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club ;) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Artistic Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* Natural whites and blacks work very well. They are the strongest source of contrast that you can access. Like soft and sharp.<br/>* This composition is an inverted triangle which is more adequate to dinamic photos where there is more visual energy flowing. Here it''s a bit unstable and uneasy.<br/><i>Cons:</i> <br/>* Failure to satisfactorily isolate the subject from the leaves in the background demands a different framing. Like <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=93756" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">kellyo</a> mentioned, a much closer crop would have solved a lot of the issues with this photo. Loosing the saturation would also work almost completely in hiding them also. Remember that presented with an almost B&amp;W scene, our eyes will scan for color.<br/> <br/><b>Technical Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* Lighting on the subject is well controlled and very even. <br/><i>Cons:</i> <br/>* There isn''t enough DOF to cover the whole subject. What little is focused is almost on the edge of the frame where the eyes don''t rest easily.<br/>* A bit more constrast to darken some visible elements in background might have given the photo more POP!<br/> <br/><b>Emotional Merit </b> <br/><i>Pros: </i> <br/>* Fresh subject. Definitely delicate and inline with the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Result </b> <br/>As you might already know, the lack of DOF is a serious issue with this picture as there is no place for the viewer''s look to rest. Adding to that the fact that the greens in the background will atract more than the few sharp but cornered drops, this isn''t a picture to look at for long.<br/>Your result does seem fair, but I''m sure you will keep doing better and better.<br/> <br/>All the best<br/>Duarte Bruno<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-02-02 11:20:45.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club ;)   Artistic Merit  Pros:  * Natural whites and blacks work very well. They are the strongest source of contrast that you can access. Like soft and sharp.* This composition is an inverted triangle which is more adequate to dinamic photos where there is more visual energy flowing. Here it''s a bit unstable and uneasy.Cons: * Failure to satisfactorily isolate the subject from the leaves in the background demands a different framing. Like  kellyo mentioned, a much closer crop would have solved a lot of the issues with this photo. Loosing the saturation would also work almost completely in hiding them also. Remember that presented with an almost B&W scene, our eyes will scan for color. Technical Merit  Pros:  * Lighting on the subject is well controlled and very even. Cons: * There isn''t enough DOF to cover the whole subject. What little is focused is almost on the edge of the frame where the eyes don''t rest easily.* A bit more constrast to darken some visible elements in background might have given the photo more POP! Emotional Merit  Pros:  * Fresh subject. Definitely delicate and inline with the challenge. Result  As you might already know, the lack of DOF is a serious issue with this picture as there is no place for the viewer''s look to rest. Adding to that the fact that the greens in the background will atract more than the few sharp but cornered drops, this isn''t a picture to look at for long.Your result does seem fair, but I''m sure you will keep doing better and better. All the bestDuarte Bruno', '2010-02-01 12:12:12.000000', '2010-02-02 11:20:45.000000', 0),
